3. Three limitations

3.1 Production use at work is paid for.

You may make production use of the Software for personal purposes, free of
charge, for as long as you like. Use in the course of employment, or in or for
a business, is not covered by that grant and needs a paid licence from the
Licensor.

Everything that is not production use stays free to everyone, at work
included: reading the source, studying it, changing it, and developing or
testing against it.

An organisation may make production use of the Software for up to thirty
calendar days to decide whether to buy a licence. The thirty days run from the
organisation's first run of the Software, they cover the organisation once
rather than once for each of its people, and no notice to the Licensor is
needed to take them.

3.2 A copy you pass on carries this licence with it.

If you give the Software to anyone, in any form, changed or unchanged, you
give it under this licence and no other, and you include this file with it.
You cannot grant anyone rights you do not hold yourself.

3.3 The notices stay.

Do not remove, hide or alter the copyright lines, this licence file, or the
statement of its licence that the Software displays in its own interface. If
you change the Software you may add your own notices, and you must not present
the result as the Licensor's work.


